1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,567
Every frame is drawn by code. A machine
cannot hear, so it was given ears.

2
00:00:05,767 --> 00:00:10,433
These captions tell you what you hear,
like someone beside you.

3
00:00:10,633 --> 00:00:15,633
Listen: horns and low strings together.
The theme climbs over three octaves.

4
00:00:15,833 --> 00:00:20,833
Red is the Hero's colour. The
brushstroke appears when the horns sing.

5
00:00:21,033 --> 00:00:26,500
Listen: a blow in mid-theme. The score
has eight horns here: eight knights.

6
00:00:26,700 --> 00:00:31,900
Listen: the violins only enter now.
In the picture, the white lines of wind.

7
00:00:32,100 --> 00:00:37,100
Listen: the brass for the first time,
fortissimo. The brass is the sun.

8
00:00:39,700 --> 00:00:44,433
The dust at the knights' feet
rises with the low accents.

9
00:00:48,180 --> 00:00:53,780
In the score, woodwinds and harps enter
for the first time: his imagination.

10
00:00:56,290 --> 00:01:01,023
The terns are the woodwinds.
Every instrument has its own character.

11
00:01:29,150 --> 00:01:32,017
The music grows towards
its first summit.

12
00:01:43,450 --> 00:01:48,783
Listen: the Hero's tender side, harps
pianissimo. They ripple in the water.

13
00:01:49,740 --> 00:01:55,140
Two red strokes: in the score two horns
sing. He has dissolved into ink.

14
00:02:07,900 --> 00:02:13,033
Strength gathers from below. He is a
knight again, and the troop marches.

15
00:02:22,700 --> 00:02:27,567
Listen: all eight horns play
the theme at full strength.

16
00:02:40,702 --> 00:02:46,702
E flat, as in the Eroica. Strauss promised
no funeral march, but plenty of horns.

17
00:02:53,130 --> 00:02:57,797
Full orchestra, fortissimo.
Ahead lies the storm mountain.

18
00:03:05,900 --> 00:03:11,300
The waterfall: in the score the harps
plunge three times, the woodwinds race.

19
00:03:34,930 --> 00:03:40,330
Listen: four times the chord breaks off
and a blow falls. Lightning, then onward.

20
00:03:51,960 --> 00:03:57,027
Listen: one huge chord that refuses
to resolve. The clouds part.

21
00:03:57,227 --> 00:04:02,393
After the chord comes the work's only
long silence: a general pause.

22
00:04:06,460 --> 00:04:11,527
Listen: a lone flute, sharp and pointed:
the mosquito, the first critic.

23
00:04:11,727 --> 00:04:16,393
Listen: the oboe mutters, rasping.
The duck, the second critic.

24
00:04:16,593 --> 00:04:21,260
Listen: two tubas a fifth apart.
The snails are Doktor Dehring.

25
00:04:21,460 --> 00:04:26,793
Listen to the rhythm: Dok-tor Deh-ring.
Parallel fifths break the pedant's rules.

26
00:04:26,993 --> 00:04:32,527
Listen: the cor anglais twists the theme,
bassoons beneath. The heron and the frogs.

27
00:04:32,727 --> 00:04:37,393
Listen: three muted trombones,
a sharp chord. The crows.

28
00:04:37,593 --> 00:04:42,593
All at once. Murk descends:
this is a fight for clarity.

29
00:04:48,650 --> 00:04:52,183
Listen: the Hero's theme in the minor,
quiet and dark.

30
00:04:57,916 --> 00:05:03,916
Whom do the tubas portray? Programme notes
say Dehring; rumour, Hanslick of Vienna.

31
00:05:04,710 --> 00:05:10,544
Into this section Strauss poured his anger
at the critics who scorned his melodies.

32
00:05:10,744 --> 00:05:17,077
Listen: muted violins rise in waves.
He looks in the pond and remembers dawn.

33
00:05:29,362 --> 00:05:33,162
The memory grows wave by wave,
with the strings.

34
00:05:54,514 --> 00:05:58,380
The morning's sun and banners,
upside down in the water.

35
00:06:12,120 --> 00:06:17,787
Listen: piccolo and flute fortissimo.
The critics attack again.

36
00:06:25,610 --> 00:06:30,343
Listen: all eight horns answer.
His comrades have come.

37
00:06:36,970 --> 00:06:40,970
His patience runs out:
the banner rises and flares.

38
00:06:53,700 --> 00:06:59,200
Listen: the brass fortissimo. He drives
the critics off, a short hymn of victory.

39
00:07:07,700 --> 00:07:13,233
Listen: solo violin, pianissimo. The
Companion: a white line without ink.

40
00:07:14,040 --> 00:07:17,573
The line rises and falls
with the violin's notes.

41
00:07:20,380 --> 00:07:25,380
Listen: the horns answer. The Hero
speaks briefly, the violin at length.

42
00:07:36,606 --> 00:07:40,606
The Companion is Pauline
de Ahna, Strauss's wife.

43
00:07:45,732 --> 00:07:51,565
Strauss called her complex,
a little coquettish, never the same twice.

44
00:07:57,900 --> 00:08:03,300
Listen: the low strings answer, softly.
The Hero's second reply.

45
00:08:45,070 --> 00:08:50,003
The violin changes mood within
a few bars, like a chameleon.

46
00:09:02,716 --> 00:09:07,983
The violin part is marked: feigned
longing, frivolity, tenderness, sarcasm.

47
00:09:29,185 --> 00:09:33,385
The shape of the line is its
mood: arcs, loops, curls.

48
00:10:01,830 --> 00:10:04,163
The sharp zigzag is sarcasm.

49
00:10:13,300 --> 00:10:17,967
The Hero's third reply, with emphasis:
he tries to win her over.

50
00:10:34,906 --> 00:10:39,239
The violin speeds up,
whirls around him and escapes.

51
00:11:37,990 --> 00:11:42,323
He flares up: his grandest gesture,
and it is the wrong one.

52
00:11:51,680 --> 00:11:55,347
Listen: the violin laughs at him,
fast and high.

53
00:12:26,066 --> 00:12:30,933
The turning point: he stops performing.
A tender, calming phrase.

54
00:12:42,390 --> 00:12:45,257
The line comes closer, turn by turn.

55
00:13:33,100 --> 00:13:37,900
Listen: G flat major, the whole
orchestra sings. The love scene.

56
00:13:38,100 --> 00:13:43,167
Listen: the harps' glissandi.
The moon's bridge glitters on the water.

57
00:13:52,283 --> 00:13:55,616
For a while, the line takes human form.

58
00:14:13,066 --> 00:14:18,466
Five years later Pauline was a solo
violin again, in the Sinfonia domestica.

59
00:14:22,092 --> 00:14:26,625
Red and white entwine,
as their themes do in the music.

60
00:14:36,534 --> 00:14:41,334
This is the slow movement:
the whole work is one vast sonata form.

61
00:14:58,810 --> 00:15:02,543
The music begins to rise, quietly,
towards its summit.

62
00:15:20,610 --> 00:15:25,810
Listen: the woodwinds break into the rise.
The adversaries knock at the door.

63
00:15:32,780 --> 00:15:36,647
Listen: the summit of love,
the Hero's theme returns.

64
00:15:38,562 --> 00:15:42,362
Purple: the first new
colour in the whole video.

65
00:15:47,235 --> 00:15:52,168
For Goethe it was the highest colour:
where red and blue meet.

66
00:16:33,630 --> 00:16:36,763
Afterglow. Quiet calls in the night.

67
00:17:00,031 --> 00:17:04,831
Two figures watch the moon,
as in a painting by Friedrich.

68
00:17:32,298 --> 00:17:36,631
Strauss wrote this work
in about three months in 1898.

69
00:17:51,854 --> 00:17:57,187
Its sister work is Don Quixote: a
ridiculous hero mirroring this noble one.

70
00:18:11,410 --> 00:18:16,810
Listen: woodwinds whisper fragments of
the critics in the silence of the night.

71
00:18:37,370 --> 00:18:42,037
Almost silence. The ink dries
and the night fades to paper.

72
00:18:51,794 --> 00:18:55,461
A sketch appears on the
paper: what is to come.

73
00:19:01,410 --> 00:19:06,610
Listen: three trumpets offstage call
to battle. Three standards.

74
00:19:20,980 --> 00:19:24,713
The orchestra answers,
and the Hero mounts his horse.

75
00:19:40,560 --> 00:19:43,560
Listen: a second call, now closer.

76
00:19:51,410 --> 00:19:54,943
Listen: the timpani strike.
The battle begins.

77
00:20:02,891 --> 00:20:08,391
To a critic of 1899 this was the
culmination of all ugliness and din.

78
00:20:10,681 --> 00:20:15,214
The white sash: the Companion
rides with him into battle.

79
00:20:20,028 --> 00:20:25,428
The critics return as whirlwinds.
Lightning and ink mark written accents.

80
00:20:40,800 --> 00:20:46,000
Side A ends mid-battle. The field
winds itself into the record's groove.

81
00:20:47,898 --> 00:20:53,165
The sound was once pressed into vinyl.
This video is a new printing of it.

82
00:20:53,475 --> 00:20:55,942
The battle continues on Side B.

